  "comments": [
    {
      "path": "tools/install-hooks.sh",
      "content": "This early return checks the effective hooks path, not whether it is configured per worktree. If `.githooks` is already set in the repository-local or global config, `npm install` skips both enabling `extensions.worktreeConfig` and writing the worktree-specific setting. The hook configuration therefore remains shared/inherited rather than isolated as required, and a later shared-config change can silently disable it in this worktree. Always perform the worktree configuration, or only skip after confirming worktreeConfig is enabled and the value exists in the worktree scope.",
      "existing_code": "if [ \"$(git rev-parse --git-path hooks 2\u003e/dev/null)\" = \".githooks\" ]; then\n  echo \"install-hooks: already enabled (core.hooksPath=.githooks)\"\n  exit 0\nfi",
      "start_line": 10,
      "end_line": 13,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "path": "tools/install-hooks.sh",
      "content": "Enabling worktreeConfig requires migrating any existing shared core.worktree and core.bare=true settings into the main worktree's config.worktree. Without that migration, Git starts applying those previously main-worktree-only settings to linked worktrees too. For example, running npm install in a worktree attached to a bare repository can leave that worktree treated as bare, breaking subsequent working-tree commands. Handle the migration before enabling the extension, or detect these configurations and skip with an actionable diagnostic rather than changing the shared configuration.",
      "existing_code": "git config extensions.worktreeConfig true \u0026\u0026 git config --worktree core.hooksPath .githooks || { echo \"install-hooks: git config failed; skipped\"; exit 0; }",
      "start_line": 14,
      "end_line": 14,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "medium"
    }
  ],
